course: dictionary
lesson: 13619
title: Indifference: not caring either way
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Indifference means not caring, or not being interested in something or someone. Look for clues in the words around it. «She shrugged with indifference when we told her the news.» A shrug is a small, bored movement, so indifference must mean she did not care much about the news.
explain: Indifference is not the same as anger or hate. Hate has strong feelings against something. Indifference has no feeling at all, neither for nor against. Try a test: swap in «did not care», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Sometimes the word is used in a formal way. «For Mia, the colour of the walls was a matter of indifference.» This means the colour made no difference to her, so she was happy with any colour.
fact: Indifference means not caring or not being interested.
fact: «Indifference» is not the same as hate: hate has strong feelings, but indifference has none.
fact: In stories, «indifference» often describes a cold or bored character.
fact: In ethics lessons, «indifference» to people who suffer is seen as a fault, because it shows no care for people in pain.
fact: «A matter of indifference» is formal: it means something that makes no difference to you either way.
exercise: «She shrugged with indifference when we told her the news.» What does indifference mean here? | *Not caring or not being interested | Very excited | Crying with joy | Very angry
exercise: Why is indifference to people who suffer seen as a fault? | Because it is a long word | *Because it shows no care for people in pain | Because it means you talk too loudly | Because it makes you very tired
exercise: «For Mia, which colour the walls were painted was a matter of indifference.» What does this tell you? | She wanted red walls most of all | She painted the walls herself | *It made no difference to her either way | She was upset about the colour
test: Which word means not caring or not being interested? | *Indifference | Curiosity | Anger | Excitement
test: «He stood in silence, showing indifference to the crowd.» What does indifference show about him? | Shouting loudly at everyone | Being very sad about the crowd | Strong love for the crowd | *No feeling, neither for nor against
exam: «The result was a matter of indifference to the team, because they would be happy either way.» What does a matter of indifference mean? | Something to be angry about | Something the team must hide | *Something that makes no difference either way | Something very important to the team
